						<h2><font size=3D"3" color=3D"#000000">
						<span style=3D"font-weight: 400">3. Daily and Weekly=20
						Planning</span></font></h2>
						<p><font size=3D"2">The Weekly Schedule is where you=20
						decide <i>when</i> you are going to work on each of your=20
						projects.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">Weekly planning=20
						gets you away from trying to FIND time for your projects=20
						and helps you MAKE time for them.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">The week is a natural period for=20
						effective short-term planning because it=92s short enough=20
						to make meaningful plans based on your top priorities,=20
						and it=92s long enough to allow you to balance urgent=20
						tasks with important long-term projects and initiatives.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">If you are familiar with the time=20
						management principle of the "big rocks" in the jar=20
						(<a href=3D"http://www.effexis.com/achieve/tour/big-rocks.htm">click =
here to learn more about=20
						it</a>), weekly planning helps you put the "big rocks" into your=20
						schedule first.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">Achieve Planner uses <b>project blocks</b>,=20
						which are times that you've committed to a specific=20
						project, to represent your big rocks. A project block is like an appo=
intment=20
						that you make with yourself to get your most important=20
						work done.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">The best way to prove to yourself that you=20
						are really committed to a project is to MAKE time for it=20
						by scheduling it in your calendar. Many Achieve Planner users report =
that=20
						scheduling project blocks really helps them get their=20
						projects done, even if they only use project blocks 2-4 hours=20
						each day.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">Weekly Planning gives Achieve Planner=20
						a significant edge over other=20
						tools that just focus on task management but don't=20
						incorporate weekly planning.</font></p>
						<p><a name=3D"WeeklyPlanning">Weekly Planning</a></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">You can use project blocks to plan as=20
						much of your time as makes sense in your work=20
						environment.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">Some people may only be able to plan=20
						the first two hours of the day reserving project blocks=20
						for their most important projects, while others may want=20
						to use project blocks to plan their entire day.</font></p>
						<p align=3D"center">
						<a target=3D"_blank" href=3D"http://www.effexis.com/achieve/screensho=
ts19/ProjectBlocksSS.png">
						<img border=3D"0" src=3D"http://www.effexis.com/achieve/screenshots19=
/ProjectBlocksSS-TN.png" width=3D"530" height=3D"376"></a><br>
						<font size=3D"1">Click Image to View Larger Size</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">In general, it is better to schedule=20
						time at the project level instead of the task level to=20
						avoid adding too much unnecessary detail to your plan.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">You'll find that focusing on a=20
						specific project for a block of time is much more=20
						effective than jumping around from one unrelated task to=20
						another. It will help you stay focused while avoiding=20
						distractions and multitasking.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">In my experience, project blocks work best when=
=20
						they are about an hour. That way you have a well=20
						defined unit of work with a clear beginning and ending...=20
						and you can stay focused without feeling too tired or=20
						distracted.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">You'll find that staying focused for a=20
						one-hour block at a time is not that difficult if you practice and=20
						keep using the system.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">If you need to, you can easily reschedule your pr=
oject=20
						blocks as needed by moving the appointments.</font></p>
			<div align=3D"center">
				<p style=3D"margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0">&nbsp;</p>
				<table border=3D"1" width=3D"490" cellspacing=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"2" i=
d=3D"table2" class=3D"testimonial">
