						<p><font size=3D"4" color=3D"#CC0000"><b>Case=20
						Study # 2 - Professor, Doctor, Researcher &amp; Working Mom</b></font=
></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">Caroline Richardson, MD is an=20
						assistant professor at the University of Michigan Health=20
						Systems, a research scientist, a family practice doctor=20
						and working mom with several projects and=20
						responsibilities for each role. </font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">=93I have a lot of different jobs:=20
						physician, researcher, teacher, mom and lots of projects=20
						going for each role. Before I started using Achieve=20
						Planner, I spent a lot of my time just trying to figure=20
						out what I should be working on and things I was=20
						supposed to be doing didn't get done.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">I went through a phase of using=20
						Microsoft Office OneNote to keep track of my stuff and=20
						it just wasn't a good enough to do list manager and=20
						project manager for me,=94 Caroline says. </font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">Since she started using Achieve=20
						Planner, Caroline has noticed some significant=20
						improvements in her organization and productivity, =93I=20
						spend less time spinning my wheels, less stuff not=20
						getting done because I forgot to do it, and more=20
						efficient planning and execution of tasks.=94</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">She adds, =93I also spend less time=20
						writing out to do lists. I feel generally calmer because=20
						the constant feeling that I have forgotten to do=20
						something important is not nagging me. I might miss a=20
						deadline or I might not have time to get something done,=20
						but at least I know exactly where I stand with every=20
						project and task and I can make rational decisions about=20
						what is most important.=94</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">=93When I'm at work, I can focus more on=20
						work stuff because I know that my list of tasks for home=20
						is up to date and that when I get off work, I will be=20
						able to efficiently tackle home tasks.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">I am more focused when working on a=20
						specific project because I am more confident the current=20
						project is the thing I need to focus on now and that the=20
						little tasks / emails / phone calls are all on a list=20
						and can be accomplished quickly later. More things are=20
						getting done and I'm less likely to miss deadlines.=94</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">Caroline found Achieve Planner's task=20
						tracking functionality and the ability to schedule time=20
						for projects particularly helpful. =93I think the task=20
						list features are really great. It is easy to add,=20
						rearrange and sort tasks. Also the idea of planning=20
						blocks of time to work on bigger projects has really=20
						helped me get the big stuff done,=94 Caroline says.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">She adds, =93Work on large complicated=20
						projects was going slowly, but the planning and=20
						organization features of Achieve Planner, especially the=20
						idea of breaking projects down into sub projects and=20
						tasks, scheduling and prioritizing each one and having a=20
						follow up or next step task for every task that has been=20
						completed is very helpful. I put in a large grant last=20
						month for which I planned and tracked every step and=20
						every revision in Achieve Planner until it was done.=94
						</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">Because Achieve Planner incorporates=20
						sound time management principles, Caroline found that=20
						just using Achieve Planner has helped her develop better=20
						time management habits, =93Because I work on so many=20
						different projects simultaneously, I was constantly=20
						switching from project to project and the transition=20
						time between projects was eating up all of my time.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">By planning large blocks of time to=20
						work on a specific project, I waste less time with=20
						frequent transitions and make more progress. Also, the=20
						organization and tracking features of Achieve Planner=20
						decrease transition times because I always know what=20
						task needs to be done next. I have several different=20
						offices and the scheduling features of Achieve Planner=20
						helped me plan my meetings etc so that I don't spend all=20
						day driving from one office to another getting nothing=20
						done.=94</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">Caroline found that using Achieve=20
						Planner helps her say =93no=94 to opportunities and=20
						commitments that don't fit with her top priorities, =93I=20
						am better able to respond to invitations and requests=20
						for collaboration or consultation because I have a=20
						better idea of how much of my time is already used up=20
						over the next few weeks. It used to be that if I got an=20
						interesting invitation I would leave it in my email=20
						inbox for a while trying to decide if I could do it. Now=20
						I say no more often and more quickly because it is more=20
						obvious that taking on another task or project is not a=20
						good idea.=94 </font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">Caroline also found the time=20
						management training and support included with Achieve=20
						Planner particularly helpful saying, =93I think the online=20
						tutorial was excellent and allowed me to get up to speed=20
						with the software very quickly.=94</font></p>
